or application within the components. Based on user feedback and the \"SPECTRUM 26\" reference, I suspect a color mapping issue. I\'m focusing on the `border-neutral-900` class and other color variables. I\'m checking that the intended color values from the HTML design are correctly applied throughout the application. I am confident in my abilities, and I am making progress.\n\n\n**Clarifying Contextual Scope**\n\nI\'ve just realized a critical misunderstanding: the \"Bold Typography\" design is *not* a standalone style. It\'s meant to be applied *to* the festival website\'s content. I need to make the festival website *look* like the \"Bold Typography\" design, while keeping its content. I\'ll re-examine the `Navbar.tsx` now.\n\n\n**Comparing Navbar and Header**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the design HTML\'s `header` element and comparing it against the existing `Navbar` component. I see the HTML `header` has a `h-40` height, which is different from the `Navbar` component\'s `h-auto`. The original design has the `h1` in the header, while in my code, the `h1` is in `Hero.tsx`. My current focus is aligning these elements based on the original design.\n\n\n**Applying Background Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now going to add a new background for the Hero section, it will contain a very large \"EXE\" text and a dark background. I\'ve realized the HTML reference does not use a radial gradient, but solid background colors. I also noticed that the original HTML included a side bar. So, I need to evaluate the styling and add the components while maintaining the application components structure.\n\n\n**Adjusting Font Implementations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a critical correction: the font families. I incorrectly set `ui-sans-serif` as the default. The intended fonts are `font-sans` (Inter or similar) and `font-mono` as a default for small text. I need to re-evaluate the font application throughout the components, especially in `Events.tsx` where I see classes like `font-mono` and `text-[11px]`. The \"font-black\" weight for headings is also a key adjustment I must make.\n\n\n**Interpreting User Feedback**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the user\'s feedback, especially the \"WHAT DID U DO\" comment, after the unintended replacement of `App.tsx` with raw HTML. I\'m focusing on their implied expectation of preserving the existing app structure, while applying the design changes, which confirms my decision to restore the original app structure in the next step. I also need to use the image references the user mentions, which are the images *in the original prompt*. I will now look at those images to clarify the correct look and design intent.\n\n\n\n**Examining Layout Structures**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the layout structure, particularly within `App.tsx`, `Navbar.tsx`, and `Hero.tsx`. I\'m starting to understand how the components fit together. The `App.tsx` has been causing problems. I will now examine how adding the router caused the styles in the `Hero.tsx` component to look bad. I\'m focusing on the border and flex properties to ensure a consistent look and feel across the app. I must ensure the overall structure in `App.tsx` correctly integrates with the styling defined elsewhere.\n\n\n**Reconstructing Image Layout**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the layout within the `Hero` component, and I\'ve identified the presence of a `Countdown` component within it. The Design HTML\'s aesthetic, with heavy borders and monospace text, aligns with the style guide. I also suspect the user\'s initial prompt likely included images that dictate the layout. I\'m prioritizing replicating the existing structure while applying these styles and also looking at restoring the original layout. I believe the Spectrum 26 website mock might provide helpful visual information.\n\n\n**Restoring Visual Details**\n\nI realized my mistake in the `Hero` component; the original design included `text-glow`, a radial gradient background, and `tracking-widest`. I had removed `text-glow` and switched to `tracking-tighter`. Now, I\'m re-implementing these elements to align with the visual style the user expects.\n\n\n**Replicating Sidebar Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the critical sidebar-main area layout from the design HTML. The `flex` properties, `aside w-72`, and the `main flex-1` are key. I need to integrate this pattern into `App.tsx` while ensuring the overall structure of the existing application is maintained. The HTML shows a hard-coded width of `w-72` for the sidebar, so I will add that. I am ensuring that I apply the correct classes in the correct elements.\n\n\n**Restructuring Component Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now integrating the design HTML\'s structural layout directly into `App.tsx`. I\'m replacing the existing top navigation with a sidebar, as the original design seems to use a header instead. I\'m focusing on the `h-screen`, border, and flex properties to correctly recreate the sidebar, header, and main content area. My goal is to use the `App.tsx` file for the overall layout.\n\n\n\n**Adapting Navigation Structure**\n\nI\'m now integrating the design HTML\'s structural layout directly into `App.tsx`, and I\'m rethinking the navigation. I\'m moving the original `Navbar` content (links to `Events`, `Schedule`, etc.) into a `Sidebar`. The original design used a header with a large title. The design HTML\'s header is `h-40`, and I need to adapt the `Navbar` to the new styling.\n\n\n**Realigning Component Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now revising the component structure, specifically focusing on `Hero.tsx`. The initial design placement of the large heading feels correct, and I\'ll now restructure the layout to reflect this. I\'ll take a look at the code. This will clarify how the new design\'s elements should integrate with the original design in the website.\n\n\n**Integrating Header Component**\n\nI\'m now rewriting the `Navbar.tsx` component to reflect the design HTML\'s header. I\'ll include the large \"SPECTRUM 26\" title, adjusting classes like `text-[140px]`, `font-black`, and `tracking-tighter` to match the visual. I\'ll maintain the existing structure, adding the necessary styling to the new code.\n\n\n**Refining Title Implementation**\n\nI\'m now integrating the design\'s large title within the `Navbar.tsx` component. I am replacing the existing navigation items with the title \"SPECTRUM 26,\" using the specified `text-[140px]`, `font-black`, and `tracking-tighter` classes. Additionally, I\'m ensuring this title is properly aligned within the new sidebar layout I\'m creating. I\'m focusing on preserving the existing structure while integrating the visual styling.\n\n\n**Integrating Sidebar Navigation**\n\nI\'m now integrating the sidebar and its navigation. The design\'s `aside` element provides a clear structure for the navigation links. I\'ll create a `Sidebar` component, or include it in `Layout` in `App.tsx`, to house the links. This will enable correct display of the system hierarchy. I will use the design HTML\'s `aside` for the sidebar, which is perfect for navigation links.\n\n\n**Implementing Sidebar Navigation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the sidebar navigation, creating `SidebarLink` components for both user and admin views, with paths that will integrate into the new `Sidebar` component. This aligns with the terminal-like design. I\'m focusing on the navigation structure and ensuring the active state highlight functions correctly.\n\n\n**Considering Mobile Adaptability**\n\nI\'m now considering the mobile view and how the sidebar navigation will adapt. The current implementation uses a fixed-width sidebar, which will likely need adjustment for smaller screens. I\'ll need to explore responsive design techniques, possibly hiding the sidebar or implementing a toggle mechanism.\n\n\n**Adapting Navigation for Mobile**\n\nI am now focusing on the sidebar\'s mobile adaptability, specifically how to ensure the navigation remains accessible on smaller screens. My plan is to keep the current structure, but I will make adjustments for mobile devices. For now, I\'ll keep the sidebar visible and stack it on mobile, but I will ensure I can implement a dropdown, if necessary. I\'ll make the changes in the `App.tsx` file for now.\n\n\n\n**Updating Footer Styling**\n\nI\'m now integrating the design HTML\'s footer styling into `Footer.tsx`. The key is to match the height, background color, border, text size, font, and uppercase styling from the HTML, which appears to match the terminal look the user is aiming for. I\'ll maintain the `flex` layout for content alignment, especially the Uptime, Mode, and Deviations, ensuring the existing structure is preserved.\n\n\n**Refining Hero Component Structure**\n\nI\'m now restructuring the `Hero.tsx` component to reflect the design HTML\'s main content. I\'m incorporating the \"Workspace Terminal\" section, keeping the `Countdown` and replacing the original title with a `Validation State`. I need to correctly apply the classes to render the new content. I\'m focusing on layout and styling.\n\n\n**Refining Hero Component Structure**\n\nI\'m now restructuring the `Hero.tsx` component to adopt the grid layout with two sections. One will render terminal content, and the other will render the validation state. I\'ll maintain the existing structure, adding the necessary styling and content to render new content.\n\n\n**Adjusting Content Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the provided HTML terminal content within the `Hero` component. I\'m focusing on the border, background, monospace font, and line breaks to match the terminal aesthetic, replacing the `Validation State` section. I\'ll preserve the grid layout for the `Countdown` component and now make adjustments to ensure the correct style for the terminal content.\n\n\n**Refining Terminal Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the terminal content from the provided HTML, specifically the \"T-Minus Launch\" and assertions. I will correctly position and style the assertions within the layout. I am keeping the countdown and its layout, and ensure that the content is correctly applied to the components.\n\n\n**Refining Countdown Component**\n\nI\'ve just successfully adapted the `Countdown` component to visually match the provided design. The styling aligns perfectly. I\'ve noted that the user wants consistent layout patterns across components. I also have to keep the overall structure. I am now evaluating the styles for `Events.tsx`.\n\n\n\n**Extracting Event Component Styling**\n\nI am now focusing on the `Events.tsx` file, specifically the `EventCard` component. The objective is to extract and apply brutalist styling to the cards. I see I must adjust `font-mono` and `text-[11px]`. The provided component has a border, background, and spacing. I will also need to re-evaluate the fonts, colors, and layout. I am keeping the original structure, replacing the current styling.\n\n\n**Refining Event Card Style**\n\nI am now implementing the brutalist style on the `EventCard` component in `Events.tsx`. The initial code\'s card styling is not brutalist; I am now applying borders, background, monospace fonts, and adjusted text sizes as per the design to achieve the desired look. I also need to ensure that the layout is consistent with other components like the `Hero` section.\n\n\n"
